prompt:
{
  "public_instructions": "You translate natural-language access-control requirements into Cedar policies using the supplied schema. Cedar denies by default. An applicable forbid overrides every permit. Guard optional attributes with has before reading them. Preserve the schema's entity, set, and attribute types. Use only the supplied task information. Do not use tools, files, search, additional model calls, or external feedback. Produce a single final answer as a JSON object with exactly one field, policy, whose value is the complete Cedar policy text. Do not include explanations or Markdown fences.
",
  "harness": {
    "instruction_blocks": [
      "Translate the access-control requirements into a complete Cedar policy set. Identify each action, eligible principal, resource type, required condition, prohibition, and exception. Use only entity types, attributes, actions, and representations supported by the schema. Implement the stated permissions and restrictions together.",
      "Before answering, check the policy mentally for both unauthorized permissions and missing required permissions. Keep distinct action rules separate when their conditions differ. Apply exceptions only to the restriction they qualify. Return one final policy set."
    ],
    "input_order": [
      "requirements",
      "schema"
    ],
    "checklist": [
      "Every required action is covered.",
      "Role, ownership, membership, and context conditions follow the requirements.",
      "Prohibitions and exceptions compose correctly.",
      "Optional attribute reads are guarded and Cedar syntax is valid."
    ]
  },
  "requirements": "The system manages outpatient controlled-substance dispensing.

The people involved are pharmacy technicians, staff pharmacists, pharmacists in charge, and prescribers. One person may hold both the staff pharmacist and pharmacist in charge role at the same time.

The resources are prescriptions and dispense records. Every prescription records its prescriber, patient, entered by, verified by, prescriber DEA, schedule level, refills remaining, and status.

Each requester carries dispense authority and a state license.

Each request carries whether PDMP was checked, whether MFA was used, whether it came from the pharmacy network, the current time, and whether emergency override was used.

A staff pharmacist or pharmacist in charge may verify a prescription only while it is in the received stage when no exception applies.

A staff pharmacist or pharmacist in charge who holds the particular exception role may verify a prescription outside the received stage.

A staff pharmacist or pharmacist in charge may verify a prescription outside the received stage when the refills remaining crosses the threshold.

A staff pharmacist and a pharmacist in charge must not verify a prescription outside the received stage when neither exception applies.

A request by a staff pharmacist to verify a prescription that does not trip any prohibition above must be able to succeed.",
  "schema": "entity Role enum ["PharmacyTechnician", "StaffPharmacist", "PharmacistInCharge", "Prescriber", "Exception"];

entity PrescriptionStatus enum ["Received", "OutsideReceived"];

entity User {
    roles: Set<Role>,
    dispenseAuthority: Bool,
    stateLicense: String,
};

entity Patient;

entity Prescription {
    prescriber: User,
    patient: Patient,
    enteredBy: User,
    verifiedBy?: User,
    prescriberDea: String,
    scheduleLevel: String,
    refillsRemaining: Long,
    status: PrescriptionStatus,
};

entity DispenseRecord;

action verifyPrescription appliesTo {
    principal: [User],
    resource: [Prescription],
    context: {
        pdmpChecked: Bool,
        mfaUsed: Bool,
        fromPharmacyNetwork: Bool,
        currentTime: datetime,
        emergencyOverrideUsed: Bool,
        refillsRemainingCrossesThreshold: Bool,
    },
};
"
}
